---
source: misc/tests/help.rs
expression: "help_output(\"findmnt\")"
---
List or find filesystems by parsing `/proc/self/mountinfo`, `/etc/fstab`, or `/proc/swaps`.

Reads the kernel's mount table by default; switch with `--fstab` or `--swaps`. Filter by `--source`,
`--target`, `--types`, and select columns with `-o`. Tree output (`--tree`) and statfs-derived
columns (SIZE/AVAIL/USED/USE%) are not yet implemented.

Usage: findmnt [OPTIONS] [DEVICE-OR-MOUNTPOINT]

Arguments:
  [DEVICE-OR-MOUNTPOINT]
          Positional fallback: a target path or source spec, equivalent to `--target` or `--source`
          respectively. Matches upstream behaviour where a bare argument is interpreted as either

Options:
  -s, --fstab
          Read /etc/fstab instead of /proc/self/mountinfo

      --swaps
          Read /proc/swaps instead of /proc/self/mountinfo

  -k, --kernel
          Read /proc/self/mountinfo (default; matches `--kernel`)

  -F, --tab-file <FILE>
          Read mount table from this file instead of the source default

  -S, --source <SPEC>
          Filter to entries with a matching source (path or LABEL=/UUID=)

  -T, --target <PATH>
          Filter to entries with a matching target path

  -t, --types <LIST>
          Comma-separated fstype filter; prefix `no` to invert (`-t nofuse,nofusectl`)

  -J, --json
          Use JSON output format

  -r, --raw
          Use raw output format

  -n, --noheadings
          Don't print headings

  -o, --output <LIST>
          Define which output columns to use

      --output-all
          Output all available columns

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
